 ## Camera and body isolation
 
+The material-rendering candidate retains the same renderer lifecycle and camera
+pose. World faces carry optional material identifiers. The GT01 adapter caches LOD 1 player and LOD 2 computer meshes on hardware, with an economy tessellation for software renderers; visual normals receive the inverse transpose of the body-height shear. GPU identity bounds software render-target dimensions without advancing or rewriting the simulation. Its resources include
+the surface shader, a static-world shadow map and the original sky texture;
+context restoration rebuilds them before presenting a frame. Drawing does not
+advance simulation time. `browser-materials.cjs` checks the actual shader path,
+whole-car visibility and loss/restoration in addition to the existing suite.
+The mountain visibility matrix compares renders with and without the complete
+player mesh at all 240 route positions. Its missing-mesh negative control must
+produce zero changed vehicle pixels. Material lighting is not checked against
+a single unlit paint RGB value.
+The finite Rendering model remains an abstraction of resource freshness, not
+a proof of lighting, material appearance or GPU shader correctness.
+
 `vehicleBodyPose()` derives presentation from the existing simulation state. Its
 impact envelope uses the existing impact decay; road pulses use simulation
 elapsed time. Drawing does not advance either. Reset/recovery clear those
@@ -72,13 +85,20 @@ Separate complete draws must show visible vehicle feedback. The suite also
 checks jump isolation, feedback direction and recovery. Existing normal-animation
 driving, occlusion, flight/landing, fall/recovery and AI tests remain required.
 
-The native flicker probe retains its 540 animation frames and all grounded
-vehicle-region thresholds. An invisible falling car is accepted only when its
-full body projection remains in view and every one of 50 vertex/face-centre
-rays meets opaque terrain first. The probe then requires actual automatic
-recovery and visible body pixels. Both renderers replay the captured deep-fall
-pose. An open-road missing-mesh negative control must still fail. This
-distinguishes legitimate terrain depth from absent or stale car rendering.
+The native flicker probe retains its 540 animation frames and all ordinary
+vehicle-region thresholds. For a terrain-hidden falling car, the full body
+projection must remain in view. Full occlusion requires every vertex/face-centre
+ray to meet opaque terrain first. Partial occlusion requires an exact car-only
+pixel-mask match against fresh renderer resources at the same frozen pose and
+backend, using the unchanged RGB-difference threshold. A tiny exposed fragment
+may cover no raster samples; its fresh reference must also produce zero pixels.
+Both cases require actual automatic recovery and visible body pixels.
+Both renderers replay the captured full falling pose, portrait and landscape
+partial poses, and the subpixel transition. Removing the entire mesh at either
+positive partial pose must yield zero pixels and fail against the fresh
+reference, as must the existing open-road missing-mesh control. This separates
+legal depth occlusion from absent or stale car rendering without inventing
+visible pixels or reducing ordinary visibility thresholds.
 
